        },
        {
            "collapsed": false,
            "id": "f353d31068",
            "info": "A clade which is noteworthy for its symbiotic relationship with plant hosts in which it uses enzyme complexes called nitrogenases to convert nitrogen into ammonia - making it more bioavailable to the plant. Plants attract rhizobia by secreting flavonoids from the root, and rhizobia track these flavonoids to find their hosts. The rhizobia in turn produce large hybrid sugar-fat molecules, which the plants recognise as signals called Nod factors, to begin building environments called nodules, in which the nitrogen-fixing bacteria enter and live, receiving sugars from the plant. In that stage of life, known as a bacteroid, the rhizobium loses much of its machinery for free-living, swelling in size, losing its shape, and making its cellular envelope more permeable, while at the same time increasing the copies of its genome to increase its protein output. Humans have long used crop rotation involving the host species of rhizobia because they empriically increased agricultural yield, even before identifying how the effect worked.",
            "name": "Rhizobium",
            "parent": "e43ee3a5ec"
        },
        {
            "collapsed": false,
            "id": "4663020b27",
            "info": "A clade of bacteria famous for its relationship to plants, in which it modifies plant genomes through horizontal gene transfer. In one well-known case, its modifications to the hormonal regulation of sweet potatoes directly influenced the human cultivation of the crop. From the 1980s onward, techniques derived from the study of agrobacteria have been staples of biotechnology, enabling impactful shifts in industrial agriculture through the creation of genetically-modified food.",
            "name": "Agrobacterium",
            "parent": "e43ee3a5ec"
        },
